server, ntdll: Add osync, in-process synchronization on macOS.

With stock Wine on macOS every wait on, and every signal of, an event,
semaphore or mutex is a request to wineserver, which costs tens of
microseconds; games make thousands a second. osync provides the in-process
synchronization interface Wine 11 added for Linux's ntsync on macOS, with
public APIs only (os_sync_wait_on_address, macOS 14.4 and later).

wineserver creates a shared memory region and passes its fd to every process
as the inproc device; objects live in 64-byte cells of it and processes name
them by generation and cell instead of by fd. Waiting takes an acquirable
object with no system call; otherwise the thread links itself into the
objects' waiter lists and sleeps on its own status word. Whoever signals an
event or semaphore hands it directly to a waiter, deciding with one
compare-and-swap that a timeout races against, and wakes it only if it
actually sleeps; a released mutex goes back to being free and one sleeping
waiter is woken to try again, so running threads don't queue behind a
sleeping one, and a waiter that keeps losing that race gets the next release
handed over, so nobody starves. PulseEvent, WaitAll and abandoned mutexes behave
as on Windows. ntdll's handle cache keeps one 64-bit value per handle (name,
access, type) with no reference count, so a thread killed mid-call can't leave
a handle value uncacheable. The server signals its own objects, and cleans up after threads
that die mid-operation or mid-wait.

It is off unless wineserver starts with WINEOSYNC=1; processes use whatever
the server provides. The design and its tests are described in the
EscApp-Runtime repository (docs/osync.md, tests/osync, tests/sync,
tests/winetest).

